WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.480
Authentication answers the question: who is this actor?

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:13.380
Login flows, password handling, MFA, account recovery, identity provider integration, session creation, and session expiration are all part of that answer.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:20.800
Weaknesses in these workflows can undermine the rest of the application because every authorization decision depends on the identity being reliable.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:31.260
Good authentication design avoids leaking whether accounts exist, protects password reset flows, rate limits sensitive actions, and uses MFA where the risk warrants it.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:38.280
Password handling should rely on established password hashing algorithms and strong operational controls rather than custom cryptography.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:46.420
The application should never store plaintext passwords, log them, place them in URLs, or send them to third-party tools unnecessarily.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:50.720
Account recovery should be treated like authentication, not like a convenience feature.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:59.340
If recovery tokens are predictable, long-lived, reusable, or exposed through logs and referrers, an attacker may not need the user password at all.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.840
Session security protects the authenticated state after login.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:11.300
Session identifiers should be unpredictable, rotated when privilege changes, invalidated on logout when appropriate, and expired according to risk.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:24.880
Cookie attributes matter: Secure helps ensure cookies are sent over HTTPS, HttpOnly helps keep scripts from reading them, and SameSite can reduce some cross-site request risks when configured for the application flow.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.820
Session management is also a workflow problem.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:37.640
Teams should consider concurrent sessions, idle timeouts, remember-me behavior, device trust, and how account recovery or password changes affect existing sessions.
